#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "054b9992-0bf6-5dcc-a04b-038ad6559959" :question/math-academy-id "e-8172" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP$, where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A$.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=4\n$$\n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=4\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=4\n$$\n\nComputing $A-4I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 4I &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nDividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161713 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "f6714653-0cfa-5049-9ae9-f921d46d03bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8173" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP$, where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the first and second columns of the matrix $P$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A$.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=8\n$$\n\nSo, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$.\n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - (-1)I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(A-(-1)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives two eigenvectors:\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne_{2} &= v_{2} - \\frac{v_{2} \\cdot e_{1}}{e_{1} \\cdot e_{1}}e_{1} \\\\\n&= v_{2} - \\frac{1}{5}e_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{e}_1$ and $\\mathbf{e}_2$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{e_{1}}{‖e_{1}‖} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\frac{e_{2}}{‖e_{2}‖} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "4779b6be-97c9-5389-b25e-e8f438f0a7fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8175" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & ∗ \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & 0 & ∗ \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D$, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$, the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that\n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1$.\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce344a63-8e04-55eb-87e3-bff4525025bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160777} {:answer/id #uuid "327cf76c-975c-5e6f-978d-d9d7ab342db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160778} {:answer/id #uuid "42d7b2d1-8355-5386-bed7-21c597a3eb64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160779} {:answer/id #uuid "4893cf95-c011-5122-94d1-4988010b724d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160780} {:answer/id #uuid "686e9341-7fcb-5b9c-a32a-de4976673d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686e9341-7fcb-5b9c-a32a-de4976673d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160781} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e7843b6-3019-518e-a98a-66735a7efe8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160776}] :question/id #uuid "c2f119be-4dbb-5583-8e23-114609166369" :question/math-academy-id "q-93726" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A = PDP^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\cdot 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D$, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77a2b06b-e239-59a5-8af0-6cbbf27c3c08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcded011-8800-520f-a693-c45cde6b8753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160927} {:answer/id #uuid "c07faf9d-7595-5927-b8d2-ba3cbfee8077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160928} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea66412-8e22-56b1-8418-fba99f7e73e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160929} {:answer/id #uuid "10aa3524-5abc-5c81-b28d-da8aadffa143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160930} {:answer/id #uuid "3be615c3-2263-5be5-ae45-ce1696d61791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea66412-8e22-56b1-8418-fba99f7e73e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160929} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fddb3a8-0b24-5c21-8b9e-79460d53daa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160926}] :question/id #uuid "64cf1141-3501-5dd0-bfea-c963489f6fe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94254" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}∗ & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ ∗ & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ ∗ & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A = PDP^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\cdot 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "611af093-6621-5224-af2a-f769c7c0e691" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b079fd-bbcc-5159-b897-a02296071f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160933} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fb0561-6a34-5c41-aed1-bd61e76a3743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160934} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3a1a82-5d2a-573d-b07f-dc28fdd6091b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160935} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc51304-8c87-5fe3-ab76-39024f9981f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160936} {:answer/id #uuid "597fa1a1-e9d1-5d71-a066-46e084ff79d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fc51304-8c87-5fe3-ab76-39024f9981f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160936} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a156a6b-3c25-5fa9-808b-9b0666d09d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160932}] :question/id #uuid "dec4c6f4-86dd-50ba-b4f8-5dac075a34ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-94260" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $D = P^{T}AP$, where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b16e6cc-b1ee-5ccc-91a7-980bf5fe50a5" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8490499a-08fe-5413-8893-bf9f8fe0a8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160939} {:answer/id #uuid "9e728b17-7829-5d52-a3b4-5d9bef430840" :answer/type {:db/ident :